  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 1232 PM MDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nSunday looks similarly wet as we are still under a favorable \nsouthwesterly trajectory for monsoon moisture and embedded \ndisturbances moving up the west side of the upper ridge. Will likely \nneed another Flash Flood Watch for the Aspen Acres burn scar as \nprobabilities of flash flood producing rain amounts are above 50% \nfor 0.25 in the NBM blends and we are still under a Slight Risk for \nexcessive rainfall. Southeasterly low level flow also looks stronger \non Sunday in some high res CAMs models which also raises concerns \nfor focused thunderstorm development across the southeast mountains. \nDeep layer of 35 kts and CAPE of 1000-2000 J/kg across the southeast \nmountains adjacent plains also suggests some higher probs of strong \nto severe storms Sunday afternoon.\n\nUpper high to the south expands westward putting southern CO under \nmore westerly flow aloft for Monday  and increasingly northwesterly \nflow through mid to late week.  Monday will be a transition day as \nprecipitable start to decrease but are still above normal.  Heavy \nrainfall will still be possible, though perhaps shunted to the \nsouthern portions of our area.  Temperatures begin to rebound a \ncouple degrees.  Tuesday looks drier for most of the area with \ntemperatures returning into the 90s across the plains.  A shortwave \ntrough drops through the central plains with variable timing \naccording to long range models but sends another cold front into the \nsoutheast plains.  This cools temperatures down a couple degrees out \neast and brings an uptick in thunderstorm chances to the southeast \nmountains and plains.  Otherwise, northwest flow continues through \nmid to late week as the upper high gradually builds and shifts over \nwestern CO/UT towards next Friday.  This suggests another warm up \nwith recycled monsoon moisture eventually returning over the \nmountains. -KT",
